r, in `docs/reviews/wi-841-done-when-blessed-in-lane/017-ADJUDICATE-263e22d.md`.\n\nEvery amended row changes what a correct implementation must do:\n\n- **LLR-262 Detail:** the loop must now bind its composed request *before* the call (in `session_body`). It must record the outcome through `record_outcome` against those bound kinds and treat the returned reason as its completion obligation. A failed call must not have its verdict read. None of this was required before.\n- **TC-326 Method:** two new tests to run.\n- **TC-327 Expected and Method:** the loop now has to bind the requested kinds too, not just the entry point, and there is one new test.\n- **LLR-278 Detail and TC-278 Expected/Method:** an act's authority is now checked per row instead of per kind. Every row the act approves needs an APPROVE ruling, and every row it re-attests needs a MEANING or CLARITY ruling. A verdict the act names must judge every row it re-attests. A new test case covers a first-approval part that returns its only row.\n- **LLR-306 Detail:** the exclusive binding must now go through the shared `bind_pending` function. An entry point that met the old text with its own writer would fail the new one.\n- **LLR-310 Detail:** both routes bind before the call, and `record_outcome` returns `(outcome, why)`. The loop takes that reason as its completion obligation, and authority is per row.\n\n**Re-attestation:** the LLR and TC rung is released, so the blessing was mine to give, and I would bless all seven new texts. The code matches them:\n- `bind_pending` is called from both `coordinator_adjudicate` (exclusive) and `agent_loop.session_body`.\n- `record_outcome` returns `(outcome, why)` and skips reading the verdict when the call failed.\n- `adjudication_bookkeeping` takes that reason as the completion obligation.\n- `kitlib.sitting` refuses any row that no accepted ruling covers.\n\nEvery test named in the TC-326 and TC-327 Methods exists in `tests/`. I didn't run the test suite; I only chec